The researchers in \cite{KlMe2009} proved that the square of the one-sided reflection coefficient $|R_+^*(k)|^2$ behaves substantially differently for small $|k|$ compared to large $|k|$, for the general coupled Davey-Stewartson II equations \begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\frac{\partial^2\phi_1}{\partial t \partial \phi_2}+\frac{\partial^2\phi_2}{\partial t \partial \phi_1}=\frac{\partial^2\phi_1}{\partial x^2}\frac{\partial\phi_2}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial^2\phi_2}{\partial x^2}\frac{\partial\phi_1}{\partial x}\\
\end{split} \label{eq:DSII} \end{equation} when solved under the given initial conditions \begin{equation}
\begin{split}
&\frac{\partial\phi_1}{\partial x}+\frac{\partial\phi_2}{\partial x}=2g_0\delta(x),\\ &\frac{\partial\phi_1}{\partial t}+\frac{\partial\phi_2}{\partial t}=2g_0\delta(t),\\ &\phi_1+\phi_2=g_0^2\delta(x-t),\\ \end{equation} denoted by $R_+^*(k)$, for small $|k|$ and large $|k|$. In particular, it was shown that the behavior of $|R_+^*(k)|^2$ changes from $O(k^{-2})$ to $O(1)$, for small $|k|$ and large $|k|$, respectively. Here, we consider an alternative interpretation of the initial conditions where the wave velocities of $\phi_1$ and $\phi_2$ are interchanged, denoted by $R_{+,v}^*(k)$. Under this interpretation, we prove that the behavior of $|R_{+,v}^*(k)|^2$ changes from $O(k^{-4})$ to $O(k^{-2})$ for small $|k|$ and large $|k|$. \end{abstract}

